`,s.jsx(x,{items:[{q:"Why does gradient descent subtract the gradient rather than add it?",a:"The gradient points in the direction of steepest ascent — the direction that increases f fastest. Training wants f (the loss) to go down, so the step goes the opposite way."},{q:"Explain backprop in one sentence using only the chain rule.",a:"The loss is a composition of layer functions, so the derivative of the loss with respect to any inner weight is the product of every local derivative between that weight and the output — backprop just evaluates that product from the output end backwards, reusing shared factors instead of recomputing them."},{q:"The gradient of a loss is exactly zero. Name three different things that could be true.",a:"A local minimum, a local maximum, or a saddle point. The first derivative cannot tell them apart; you need the second derivative (curvature) to classify which one you are sitting on."},{q:"Where in the chain-rule product does the vanishing-gradient problem come from?",a:"Backprop multiplies one local derivative per layer. If those factors are consistently below 1 — as saturated sigmoid or tanh derivatives are — the product shrinks exponentially with depth, so early layers receive almost no signal. Factors above 1 give the mirror-image explosion."}]}),`
